  "narrative": "I assessed all newly delivered discovery results. For direction +1 claims, positive signed estimates mean weaker dependency; for direction -1 claims, positive signed estimates mean stronger dependency. I continue to use an approximate 0.1 absolute dependency-score difference as a benchmark for scientifically meaningful magnitude, while acknowledging that the outcomes are continuous CRISPR scores rather than literal relative percent outcomes.  H56 tests KEAP1 loss as a predictor of stronger STAT3 dependency: signed +0.016 (95% -0.012 to +0.044). This is very small and cannot support a meaningful association; I reject and close H56. H57 tests TP53 loss as a predictor of stronger STAT3 dependency: signed -0.012 (95% -0.037 to +0.012). This is near null and the interval is far below the meaningful benchmark; I reject and close H57.  H65 tests MTAP loss as a predictor of weaker MDM2 dependency: signed -0.019 (95% -0.045 to +0.008). The result is near null and opposite to the registered direction, so I reject H65. H66 tests MTAP loss as a predictor of weaker USP7 dependency: signed -0.013 (95% -0.057 to +0.031). This is also null/opposite and below any meaningful threshold; I reject H66.  H67 and H68 estimate the TP53-loss effect on USP7 directly within MTAP-loss and MTAP-intact cells, respectively. In MTAP-loss cells, H67 gives signed +0.284 (95% +0.105 to +0.463); in MTAP-intact cells, H68 gives signed +0.421 (95% +0.343 to +0.498). Because these are direction +1 claims, positive values mean TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency. Both intervals exclude zero and remain at or above the 0.1 benchmark, so I accept H67 and H68 as dataset-specific stratum associations and close their investigations. These results reinforce H11: the TP53-loss USP7 association does not require MTAP-loss context. They also make the previously unresolved MTAP interaction less central, but H67/H68 are not held-out independent validation because they reuse discovery observations.  H69 is the most interesting non-accepted result. The registered claim was that CDKN2A loss would produce weaker MDM2 dependency among TP53-intact cells, direction +1. The signed estimate was -0.119 (95% -0.158 to -0.079), i.e., the opposite direction: CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency. I reject the original H69 direction because the evidence is opposite and the interval excludes the originally claimed weaker-dependency direction. I do not immediately accept the reversed association: the point estimate is only moderately above the 0.1 benchmark, the interval lower bound is 0.079, and there is substantial multiplicity plus possible confounding because 9p21 CDKN2A deletions can co-occur with MTAP loss and broader 9p alterations. I therefore registered a linked reversed proposal for CDKN2A loss as a predictor of stronger MDM2 dependency in TP53-intact cells, with initial status unresolved for later evidence review.  H70 tests STK11 loss as a predictor of stronger KRAS dependency: signed -0.002 (95% -0.022 to +0.018), essentially null; I reject and close H70. H71 tests NF1 loss as a predictor of stronger KRAS dependency: signed -0.020 (95% -0.049 to +0.009). This is small and opposite to the anticipated stronger-dependency direction; I reject and close H71. H74 tests MSI-high as a predictor of stronger DHX9 dependency: signed +0.017 (95% -0.017 to +0.052). This is small and well below the benchmark, supporting outcome specificity of the accepted MSI-high WRN association; I reject and close H74. H75 tests SMARCA4 loss as a predictor of stronger POLR2A dependency: signed -0.005 (95% -0.024 to +0.014). This supports outcome specificity of the accepted SMARCA2 association; I reject and close H75. H76 tests residual ARID1A-loss effects on SMARCA2 dependency within SMARCA4-loss cells: signed +0.034 (95% -0.013 to +0.082). The upper bound is below the 0.1 benchmark and the interval includes zero, so there is no strong evidence for additional ARID1A co-dependency beyond the dominant SMARCA4-loss effect; I reject and close H76.  The highest-priority validation request is H20. H20 is accepted, huge in discovery, internally robust in ARID1A-loss, ARID1A-intact, and Avana-library subsets, but not yet independently validated. Validating it is the most informative use of the one requested validation this iteration.",
  "research_notes": "Round-5 appraise update. The accepted evidence base is still dominated by four large dataset-specific associations: MSI-high -> stronger WRN (H10, validated), KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 (H13, validated), TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 (H11, validated), and SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 (H20, accepted but not yet held-out validated). New subgroup robustness evidence accepts H67/H68: TP53-loss weaker USP7 holds in MTAP-loss and MTAP-intact strata, though the MTAP-loss effect is smaller (+0.284 vs +0.421). New nulls support outcome specificity and refute several mechanistic guesses: KEAP1/TP53 effects do not move STAT3 (H56/H57), MTAP main effects do not move MDM2/USP7 (H65/H66), STK11/NF1 do not move KRAS (H70/H71), MSI-high does not move DHX9 (H74), SMARCA4 loss does not move POLR2A (H75), and ARID1A loss adds little within SMARCA4-loss cells (H76). H69 was rejected for its registered direction because CDKN2A loss in TP53-intact cells appeared to be associated with stronger, not weaker, MDM2 dependency. A linked reversed proposal was registered as unresolved; it is near/borderline at the ~0.1 benchmark and could be confounded by 9p21 deletion patterns or multiple testing, so do not accept without later scrutiny or validation. I requested independent validation of H20 because it is the only accepted major association without held-out validation. After validation returns, reassess H20 explicitly. Next useful analyses likely include the reversed CDKN2A-MDM2 proposal and untested broad probes H72/H73 if still informative. All conclusions remain associations in the supplied dataset, not causal or generalizable claims."
